<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/laptop-work.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/laptop-work-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/laptop-work.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal terms A–Z</span></nav><p class="kicker">Searchable glossary</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Terms A–Z</h1><p class="lede">44 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually calculated as replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay this amount first.</dd><dt>Additional living expense</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra costs of living elsewhere, such as lodging and meals above normal, while a home is unlivable after a covered loss. Often part of Coverage D.</dd><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision that sets the amount of loss, signed by any two of the three panel members: the two appraisers and the umpire.</dd><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of a covered loss.</dd><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>A written notice from the policyholder or insurer invoking the appraisal clause and starting the process.</dd><dt id="g-C">Claim number</dt><dd>The identifier an insurer assigns to a reported loss. It belongs on every letter, estimate, and invoice tied to the claim.</dd><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>The dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy, which pays for damage to the house and attached structures.</dd><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, for detached garages, sheds, fences, and similar buildings on the property.</dd><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage, for belongings such as furniture, clothing, and electronics.</dd><dt>Coverage D</dt><dd>Loss of use coverage, which typically pays additional living expense or lost rental income after a covered loss.</dd><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property, coverages, limits, deductibles, and policy period.</dd><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss the policyholder pays before the insurer pays. It may be a flat amount or a percentage.</dd><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear, and condition. Insurers subtract it to reach actual cash value.</dd><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm produced by a line of fast-moving thunderstorms, capable of damage across many counties.</dd><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial or personal stake in the outcome beyond a reasonable fee, as most appraisal clauses require.</dd><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof eaves and rakes to direct water into gutters and away from the fascia and decking.</dd><dt id="g-E">Estimate</dt><dd>An itemized statement of the work and materials needed to repair a loss, with quantities and prices for each line.</dd><dt>Exclusion</dt><dd>A policy provision that removes certain causes of loss or types of property from coverage, such as flood on a standard homeowners policy.</dd><dt id="g-H">Hail strike</dt><dd>A point of impact from a hailstone, often seen as a bruise, fracture, or loss of granules on shingles or a dent in metal.</dd><dt id="g-I">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof membrane installed under shingles at eaves, valleys, and penetrations to resist ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice that forms at a roof edge when melting snow refreezes, backing water up under shingles and into the home.</dd><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate describing one task or material, with a quantity, unit, and price.</dd><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs reasonably match the rest of the surface in color, texture, or style.</dd><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps taken after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or drying out a wet basement.</dd><dt id="g-N">NFIP</dt><dd>The National Flood Insurance Program, the federal program that writes most flood insurance policies in the United States.</dd><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired or rebuilt property up to current building codes.</dd><dt>Overhead and profit</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for business overhead and profit, often 10% each in estimates, when a loss requires coordination of multiple trades.</dd><dt id="g-P">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount, common for wind and hail on some policies.</dd><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>The steepness of a roof, expressed as rise over 12 inches of run. Steeper roofs usually cost more to repair.</dd><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</dd><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld from an initial payment that the insurer pays later, usually once repairs are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd><dt>Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without subtracting depreciation.</dd><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of all damage and the work needed to repair it. Many disputes come down to differences in scope.</dd><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of accumulated snow and ice on a roof. Heavy loads can crack trusses or collapse structures.</dd><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of roofing measure equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd><dt>Subrogation</dt><dd>The insurer’s right, after paying a claim, to recover its payment from a third party who caused the loss.</dd><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy provision, together with state law, that limits how long a policyholder has to file a lawsuit over a claim.</dd><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for damage or costs found after the original estimate, often during repairs.</dd><dt>Surplus lines</dt><dd>Insurance written by carriers not licensed as standard insurers in the state, often used for hard-to-place risks.</dd><dt id="g-T">Tear-off</dt><dd>Removal of existing roofing layers down to the decking before new roofing is installed.</dd><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd><dt id="g-W">Waste factor</dt><dd>An added percentage of material in an estimate to account for cutting and fitting, especially for roofing and siding.</dd><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>A widely used estimating software program that insurers and contractors use to price repairs with regional unit costs.</dd></dl><p class="gloss-none" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
